      "transcript": "Tabitha Crowle: Welcome to Bramley Signal Box. Keep the plan in front of you. You entered from the south, and reception is the first marked point on the left, at A. The doorway immediately beside it is staff-only, so follow the visitor symbol rather than the historic sign above the door.\nTabitha Crowle: From reception the level route turns east. The accessible ramp is at F, beside the newer glazed door. The stone steps farther north are part of the original building but are not on the public route. The ramp has handrails on both sides and reaches the same landing as the lift.\nTabitha Crowle: Continue west across the central hall. The viewing platform is C. Its dark non-slip floor marks the safe stopping line, and it lets visitors see the machinery without entering the working area. The nearby open doorway is only a store, so do not treat it as another viewing point.\nTabitha Crowle: Behind the platform is the lever frame, marked H. It contains the long painted levers once used to control points and signals. Several still connect to preserved equipment below the floor, which is why visitors must remain behind the brass rail.\nTabitha Crowle: The token room is D, opposite the information point. Its small instruments once controlled access to a single-track section. Although the room looks like an office on the plan, its public entrance is clearly marked from the main passage.\nTabitha Crowle: Visitors who need a quiet pause can use the bench at B, across the path from reception. It is screened from the busiest circulation route but remains visible to staff. Please leave the space free for anyone who needs to sit rather than using it to store bags.\nTabitha Crowle: Now for today’s activities. The morning signal demonstration takes place at the lever frame, option E on your activity list. It begins at ten fifteen. The guide moves only three selected levers; the rest are secured. Photography is permitted without flash, and bags must stay beneath the bench so the narrow aisle remains clear.\nTabitha Crowle: The token exchange demonstration is in the token room, option A. The guide uses replicas to show how a physical token prevented two trains from entering the same section. Original instruments stay behind glass, but the demonstration follows the historical sequence closely.\nTabitha Crowle: The family activity is in the retired carriage, option D. Children can sort replica tickets and build a simple timetable there. It is close to the token room, but the two sessions have different meeting points and are intended for different audiences.\nTabitha Crowle: Finally, the accessible tour starts at the viewing platform, option C. Everyone can gather there without blocking reception, and the guide can check the lift and hearing loop before the group moves on. The general tour starts elsewhere, so arrive five minutes early and wait on the platform.\nTabitha Crowle: A few practical points before you set off: the working floor closes before the shop, step-free parking must be booked in advance, and late arrivals should wait at reception rather than crossing a demonstration route. The visitor plan sometimes looks less direct than the old staff passages, but its route protects both people and the operating machinery.\nTabitha Crowle: The labels on the building deserve a final word. Some name the room’s railway purpose, while the plan names its current visitor function. Use the plan for navigation and the historic labels for interpretation. Volunteers in blue badges can explain any difference. At closing time, follow the illuminated visitor arrows; staff may secure the working floor before the rest of the site, and a route that was open in the morning may then be unavailable. Printed large-type guides are available beside reception.",
