      "transcript": "Noemi Farrow: I moved our first water-sampling site about two hundred metres upstream. After last week’s rain, the bank at the original point collapsed, and it is no longer safe to stand there.\nKieran Moss: Does the replacement still give us a fair comparison?\nNoemi Farrow: Yes. Flow, depth and shade are close to the original conditions. We measured the depth and photographed the canopy rather than simply assuming that the sites were alike.\nKieran Moss: I understand the site change. What exactly does the field blank test?\nNoemi Farrow: We take sterile water into the field, open it beside the real samples and handle it with the same equipment. If DNA appears in that blank, contamination was probably introduced during field handling rather than coming from the river.\nKieran Moss: So it travels in the same cooler and stays open for the same length of time, but never touches river water.\nNoemi Farrow: Exactly. If it is positive, we review gloves, bottle handling and work surfaces before interpreting the river samples.\nKieran Moss: For labels, could we just write the river name on each lid?\nNoemi Farrow: No, lids can become separated from bottles. We’ll use waterproof labels with coded numbers, then copy every code into the field log. One person will read the code aloud while the other checks the entry.\nKieran Moss: And the code should identify the site, replicate and time without revealing which species we expect.\nNoemi Farrow: Right. We also change gloves between sites and after handling ropes or measuring equipment, even when the gloves still look clean.\nKieran Moss: If all three bottles give a negative result, can we report that the species is absent?\nNoemi Farrow: No. DNA may be present below the detection limit or distributed unevenly. We should report non-detection and the number of replicates, not turn an uncertain result into proof of absence.\nKieran Moss: Let’s check the controls for the second question block. How are we reducing contamination from boats?\nNoemi Farrow: We’ll lower the bottle from a clean launch point upstream of the moorings, where ropes and hull wash are less likely to affect the sample.\nKieran Moss: The field blank covers handling. What checks the laboratory stage?\nNoemi Farrow: An extraction blank. It enters when the laboratory reagents are added, so a signal there points to contamination introduced during extraction rather than at the river.\nKieran Moss: Weather and water level could still differ between sites.\nNoemi Farrow: That is why both sites will be sampled within a matched tidal window. It will not remove every environmental difference, but it limits variation caused by changing water volume.\nKieran Moss: What about the journey back to the laboratory?\nNoemi Farrow: We’ll use chilled transport: the bottles remain cool but are not frozen, and the courier time is recorded.\nKieran Moss: Finally, how many samples at each point?\nNoemi Farrow: Triplicate samples. Three independent bottles help us recognise an anomalous result, and we can report how many replicates were positive rather than only a yes-or-no conclusion.\nKieran Moss: I’ll print the labels, calibrate the meter and confirm collection. You’ll prepare sterile bottles and the blanks. At each site, one person handles the pole while the other records time, rainfall, boat traffic and water conditions.\nNoemi Farrow: We should also pre-register when an extraction will be repeated. Making that decision only after seeing a weak signal would invite bias. A short pilot at the campus pond can rehearse the sequence, but it will not enter the dataset.\nKieran Moss: The report also needs a clear distinction between detecting target DNA and demonstrating a living local population. Water, birds or equipment can move genetic material beyond the place where the organism lives.\nNoemi Farrow: Agreed. We’ll interpret the result alongside flow, recent rainfall and boat traffic, and we’ll publish the quality-control outcomes even if they complicate the story. Excluding an inconvenient blank after seeing the results would make the design impossible to audit.\nKieran Moss: Back in the laboratory, filenames should preserve the field codes rather than replace them with species names. That keeps analysts blind to our expectation during extraction and makes every result traceable to its bottle, time and replicate. We will retain the original log sheets after transcription, record every correction rather than overwriting it, and reconcile bottle counts before opening the cooler. If a seal is broken or a code cannot be matched, the sample will be quarantined and reported as excluded instead of silently reassigned.",
