      "passage": "A\nEvaporative clay coolers exploit a familiar physical change: liquid water absorbs heat as it becomes vapour. In a common design, a smaller porous clay vessel sits inside a larger one, with wet sand packed into the gap. Food is placed in the inner pot, the opening is covered with a damp cloth, and the whole device stands in shade. Moving air carries water vapour away from the outer surface. Although the principle is old, performance depends on details such as wall thickness, vessel geometry and the area exposed to the air; a locally successful design cannot simply be copied at another scale and expected to behave identically. The two vessels should not touch, because direct contact creates an uncontrolled route for heat. A narrow, evenly packed gap makes later comparisons more meaningful.\n\nB\nPorosity is essential. Water must migrate slowly through the outer wall before it can evaporate, so a glazed vessel performs poorly even if the sand inside remains wet. At the other extreme, clay with very large pores may leak faster than evaporation can use the water. Makers therefore test vessels before pairing them. The wet sand serves as a reservoir and maintains contact around the inner chamber, but flooding the gap is unnecessary and may waste water. The aim is a controlled supply to the surface, not a permanently overflowing container.\n\nC\nThe surrounding atmosphere determines how rapidly that surface water can leave. Steady airflow replaces moist air close to the pot with drier air, whereas still conditions allow a humid boundary layer to form. High humidity reduces cooling because air already near saturation can accept little additional vapour. This explains why the same cooler may achieve a useful temperature difference on a dry afternoon yet show little change after rain. Measurements of air temperature and relative humidity are consequently needed whenever results from different days or regions are compared.\n\nD\nLocation can either support or defeat the mechanism. Direct sunshine adds heat to the vessel, while an enclosed room restricts the movement of air; neither is suitable. A shaded veranda with cross-ventilation is usually better. Raising the pot on a stable stand improves airflow beneath it and prevents water pooling around the base. Very strong wind is not automatically beneficial, however, because it can raise consumption beyond what a household can replenish. Placement therefore involves a balance between ventilation, protection from radiant heat and practical access to water. Wind exposure should be assessed where the cooler will actually be used, rather than at a distant weather station whose buildings and vegetation may create different conditions.\n\nE\nA clay cooler is not a refrigerator. Its internal temperature follows weather conditions and does not remain at the consistently low level required for meat, dairy products or medicines. It may nonetheless slow wilting and moisture loss in some fruit and vegetables. The benefit varies with crop type, initial condition and local climate. Responsible descriptions state those limits: reducing produce temperature by several degrees may extend freshness, but it is not equivalent to meeting a regulated cold-storage threshold.\n\nF\nRoutine care is simple but consequential. Clean water reduces deposits; a damp cloth over the opening excludes many insects; and washing the inner chamber prevents food residues from accumulating. In hard-water areas, minerals can gradually block the pores, so gentle brushing is preferable to detergents that may leave their own residue. Cracks require attention because a vessel that leaks rapidly no longer cools evenly. Between uses, the food chamber should dry fully, and the cover should be washed rather than merely re-wetted.\n\nG\nWhere electricity is unreliable, repairability may be as important as cooling capacity. The vessels can often be made locally, users can see when water needs replacing, and a damaged outer pot can be changed without discarding the inner one. Yet this simplicity also exposes a constraint: the system consumes water and demands daily attention. In a persistently humid or water-scarce place, those requirements may outweigh the benefit. Calling the design ‘off-grid’ therefore does not establish that it is appropriate for every household beyond the electricity network.\n\nH\nA fair trial records internal and ambient temperatures, humidity, airflow and the condition of comparable batches of produce over time. Researchers also measure water consumption and distinguish an immediate temperature drop from an actual extension of storage life. A single cool reading or striking photograph is weak evidence because weather and starting quality can dominate the result. Replicated household trials weigh produce at intervals, use a defined spoilage scale and report bruising present at the outset. This fuller record allows users to judge a practical trade-off among freshness, water use and maintenance instead of treating the lowest temperature observed as the only outcome. Investigators also document how often water is added and who performs the maintenance. A device that works only under unusually attentive supervision may be unsuitable in routine household use.",
