      "passage": "A\nA deterministic weather forecast presents one simulated future from a selected estimate of the atmosphere’s current state. Ensemble prediction instead runs many related simulations, varying starting conditions, uncertain model processes or even the models themselves. The ensemble spread is useful information because observations and equations are imperfect. Perturbations remain small: the purpose is to sample plausible uncertainty around the present atmosphere, not to compare unrelated weather situations. The distribution of outcomes shows where apparently minor differences grow into materially different forecasts.\n\nB\nWhen members remain close, forecasters may have greater confidence in the broad evolution shown; when they diverge, the atmosphere may be near a sensitive transition. Neither pattern is a guarantee. Tightly clustered members can share a model error, so low spread sometimes gives false confidence. Forecast centres compare past spread with past error to test whether apparent certainty has been justified. This process exposes structural weaknesses that adding more nearly identical simulations would not solve. Forecasters also inspect whether spread grows at a realistic rate through the forecast period. A system that remains artificially narrow can appear decisive while systematically under-representing uncertainty.\n\nC\nFor many decisions, probabilities are more useful than a single most-likely value. A transport manager considering disruptive snow faces different costs from a walker deciding whether to carry a coat. Even when both receive the same probability, their thresholds for action differ. A wide ensemble spread may indicate sensitivity or insufficient evidence, while the resulting forecast probabilities let users combine likelihood with consequence. The model does not choose the action; it supplies a structured description of uncertainty for the decision-maker.\n\nD\nCommunicating that description is difficult. Some users mistake a low probability for impossibility; others treat any mention of danger as certainty. Cones, plumes and percentile ranges can display variation, but viewers need to know which interval is shown and whether rare outcomes fall beyond it. Calibrated verbal labels may help, yet no graphic removes judgement. Effective communication pairs the probability with the potential impact and states what remains outside the displayed range.\n\nE\nEnsemble size influences how well rare outcomes are sampled, but more members are not the only route to improvement. Better observations, more realistic physical processes and correction of systematic bias may change reliability more than another set of runs. Computing resources impose a trade-off: a centre can increase the number of members or devote more power to finer resolution that represents terrain and storms. The best allocation depends on the forecast purpose and on which source of error currently dominates.\n\nF\nVerification asks whether issued probabilities match observed frequencies over many cases. If events assigned a twenty per cent probability occur about once in five, that category is calibrated. Calibration alone is insufficient, because a forecast that always repeats the climatic average can be reliable yet uninformative. Analysts also examine sharpness—the justified ability to move probabilities away from that average. A useful system combines calibration with discrimination rather than protecting its record through permanently cautious forecasts. Verification is divided by season, lead time and event type because good average performance can conceal weakness in precisely the situations that matter most to a user.\n\nG\nUncertainty should be exposed and used, not hidden behind one line on a map. This does not mean that every member deserves identical operational weight. Forecasters inspect clusters, current observations and known regional biases. Expert interpretation is essential when measurements conflict with simulations or when a model weakness affects the event of interest. The aim is not to select a favourite member but to explain how each body of evidence changes the distribution of plausible outcomes.\n\nH\nEnsembles are decision tools, not collections of rival prophecies. Their value depends on technical quality and on whether users connect probabilities with impacts and decision thresholds. Sector-specific systems increasingly estimate outcomes such as a road closure rather than snowfall alone, but that translation adds assumptions about exposure and vulnerability that also require verification. Transparent products state where model output ends and impact modelling or expert judgement begins. Archives of forecasts and outcomes then allow users to examine routine misses as well as memorable successes, making uncertainty available for disciplined action rather than disguising it. Training is most effective when users practise with archived cases, choose an action before seeing the outcome and then discuss why the same probability supported different defensible decisions.",
